public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync {

    private static final Integer MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION = 100;

    /**
     * Callouts are not allowed directly from triggers, so the callouts are made
     * asynchronously after the transaction commits. One request is still sent per
     * new account, per the requirement that the ERP team can trace every account
     * on its own. A single future transaction is capped at 100 callouts, so large
     * marketplace batches (up to 200 accounts) are split into chunks of 100.
     */
    public static void syncNewAccounts(List<Account> newList) {
        if (newList == null || newList.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Account> all = new List<Account>(newList);
        Integer index = 0;
        while (index < all.size()) {
            List<Account> chunk = all.subList(
                index,
                Math.min(index + MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION, all.size())
            );
            sendChunk((List<Account>) chunk);
            index += MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION;
        }
    }

    @future(callout=true)
    private static void sendChunk(List<Account> accounts) {
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            Map<String, Object> body = new Map<String, Object>{
                'salesforceId' => acc.Id, // 18-char Id in triggers
                'name'         => acc.Name,
                'country'      => String.isNotBlank(acc.BillingCountry)
                                    ? acc.BillingCountry
                                    : null
            };

            HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
            req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
            req.setMethod('POST');
            req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
            req.setBody(JSON.serialize(new List<Map<String, Object>>{ body }));

            new Http().send(req);
        }
    }
}
```

Note on deviation: the ticket asked for the callout "right from the trigger", but Salesforce does not permit synchronous callouts from triggers (as the failing tests confirmed). The callouts are therefore made from a `@future(callout=true)` method, queued after the DML transaction commits. It still sends one request per account as required; batches larger than 100 accounts are split into multiple future transactions to respect the 100-callout-per-transaction limit.

