    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task for each opportunity in the weekly pipeline review.
     *
     * Tasks are inserted with allOrNone=false: a Task rejected by a validation
     * rule does not stop the others from being created. The Ids of the
     * opportunities whose Task could not be created are returned.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities to create follow-ups for.
     * @return Set of opportunity Ids for which the follow-up Task was NOT created.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedIds;
        }

        // Preserve order and avoid duplicates when the same Id is passed twice.
        List<Id> distinctIds = new List<Id>(new Set<Id>(opportunityIds));

        Map<Id, Opportunity> opps = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN :distinctIds]
        );

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        Map<Integer, Id> taskIndexToOppId = new Map<Integer, Id>();
        for (Opportunity opp : opps.values()) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId = opp.Id,
                OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                Status = 'Not Started',
                Priority = 'Normal'
            ));
            taskIndexToOppId.put(tasks.size() - 1, opp.Id);
        }

        // Insert in chunks of 200 so we never exceed the per-statement record
        // limit, while keeping the total DML statement count very low.
        Integer offset = 0;
        for (Integer start = 0; start < tasks.size(); start += 200) {
            List<Task> chunk = tasks.subList(start, Math.min(start + 200, tasks.size()));
            List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(chunk, false);
            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    failedIds.add(taskIndexToOppId.get(offset + i));
                }
            }
            offset += chunk.size();
        }

        // Any requested Id that no longer exists (or wasn't accessible) also failed.
        for (Id oppId : distinctIds) {
            if (!opps.containsKey(oppId)) {
                failedIds.add(oppId);
            }
        }

        return failedIds;
    }
}
```

**What changed and why:** The per-record `insert` inside the try/catch blew the 150-DML-statement governor limit at 200 opportunities (test setup DML + 200 task inserts). I switched to `Database.insert(chunk, false)` with `allOrNone=false`, which preserves the required behavior exactly — a validation-rule rejection on one Task does not stop the others, and the failed opportunities are still identified via the per-record `SaveResult` — but does it in one DML statement per 200 tasks instead of one per task. Chunks of 200 keep the method safe if the list ever exceeds the per-statement record limit.

