public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {
    /**
     * Before insert: when a new line's Discount is null, set it to the
     * standard discount for its product. Lines where the rep typed a
     * discount (including 0) are left untouched.
     *
     * Bulk safe: one query for all products, regardless of how many
     * lines are in the transaction (e.g. 200-line CPQ quotes).
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> lines) {
        if (lines == null || lines.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Collect the products we need standard discounts for.
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : lines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }
        if (productIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // One query for all products; the discount *rules* themselves stay
        // in PricingRules (standardDiscountForFamily is query-free).
        Map<Id, Product2> productsById = new Map<Id, Product2>(
            [SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds]
        );

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : lines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                Product2 product = productsById.get(line.Product2Id);
                String family = (product != null) ? product.Family : null;
                line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**What changed and why:** `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)` runs a SOQL query per call, so calling it once per line hit the 100-query governor limit on the 200-line CPQ test. The handler now issues a **single** bulk query for the products' `Family` values and calls the query-free `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)` — so the discount rules themselves still live only in `PricingRules`, nothing was copied, and the trigger is fully bulk-safe. (Longer-term I'd still suggest the pricing team add a bulk-safe overload like `standardDiscountFor(Set<Id>)` so callers don't need their own query at all.)

