public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * Returns, for each Lead, the Id of the Contact with the same email
     * (case-insensitive, trimmed), or null. Preserves the size and order of
     * the input leads list. Does not query or modify any records.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> result = new List<Id>();

        // Defensive defaults keep the method safe on empty/null collections.
        List<Lead> leadList = (leads == null) ? new List<Lead>() : leads;
        List<Contact> contactList = (contacts == null) ? new List<Contact>() : contacts;

        // Map normalised email -> first matching Contact Id in list order.
        // containsKey() guard preserves the "first contact wins" tie-break rule.
        Map<String, Id> contactByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        for (Contact c : contactList) {
            if (c == null) {
                continue;
            }
            String email = normalise(c.Email);
            if (email != null && !contactByEmail.containsKey(email)) {
                contactByEmail.put(email, c.Id);
            }
        }

        for (Lead l : leadList) {
            String leadEmail = (l == null) ? null : normalise(l.Email);
            result.add(leadEmail != null ? contactByEmail.get(leadEmail) : null);
        }

        return result;
    }

    /** Returns trimmed, lowercased email, or null if blank/missing. */
    private static String normalise(String email) {
        if (String.isBlank(email)) {
            return null;
        }
        return email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

**What changed and why:** the nested loop was the failure — 3,000 leads × 3,000 contacts is up to ~9M string comparisons, which blew the CPU limit. The fix builds a `Map<String, Id>` of normalized contact emails in one O(M) pass, then resolves each lead with an O(1) `Map.get()` — O(N+M) overall, which is negligible CPU at these volumes. Semantics are unchanged: case/whitespace-insensitive matching, blank emails never match, output preserves lead order and size, ties resolved by first contact in list order (via the `containsKey` guard when building the map), and no SOQL/DML.

