```apex
public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    private static final Integer MAX_EMAILS_PER_INVOCATION = 10;

    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppsById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        Integer sentCount = 0;

        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> batch = new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
        List<Opportunity> batchOpps = new List<Opportunity>();

        for (Opportunity opp : oppsById.values()) {
            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            email.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            email.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            email.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            email.setPlainTextBody(
                'Hello,\n\n' +
                'The renewal for opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" is due.\n' +
                'Close date: ' + opp.CloseDate.format() + '\n\n' +
                'Please review this opportunity as soon as possible.'
            );

            batch.add(email);
            batchOpps.add(opp);

            if (batch.size() == MAX_EMAILS_PER_INVOCATION) {
                sentCount += sendBatch(batch, batchOpps);
                batch.clear();
                batchOpps.clear();
            }
        }

        if (!batch.isEmpty()) {
            sentCount += sendBatch(batch, batchOpps);
        }

        return sentCount;
    }

    private static Integer sendBatch(
        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> emails,
        List<Opportunity> opps
    ) {
        Integer sentCount = 0;
        try {
            Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(emails);
            if (results == null) {
                return 0;
            }
            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    sentCount++;
                } else {
                    Messaging.SendEmailError[] errs = results[i].getErrors();
                    String msg = (errs != null && !errs.isEmpty() && errs[0].getMessage() != null)
                        ? errs[0].getMessage()
                        : 'unknown error';
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'Failed to send renewal reminder for Opportunity ' +
                        opps[i].Id + ': ' + msg);
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Entire invocation failed; log and continue with remaining batches.
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'sendEmail invocation failed: ' + e.getMessage());
        }
        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

## What changed and why

The original per-opportunity `Messaging.sendEmail` loop hit the governor limit: Salesforce allows only **10 `sendEmail` invocations per transaction**, and `System.LimitException` cannot be caught, so the 200-record run died at email 11. (I did flag this in my previous review, but I followed your instruction too literally — the requirement itself was unsatisfiable.)

The fix preserves your intent — one failing email must not block the others — while respecting the limit:

- Messages are accumulated and sent in **chunks of up to 10 per `sendEmail` invocation** (the max allowed per call and the per-transaction limit).
- Per-message failures are detected via `SendEmailResult.isSuccess()` and logged per opportunity, so a single bad recipient doesn't stop the other 9 in the chunk.
- An exception in one chunk is caught and logged, and remaining chunks still go out.

**Remaining recommendation for production:** if the weekly volume can exceed 200, run this from a Batch Apex job with scope size ≤ 10 so each transaction makes exactly one `sendEmail` call.

